<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope Innocent IV</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1195–<a href="/years/1254">1254</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Sinibaldo Fieschi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pope Innocent IV reigned from 1243 to 1254. At the First Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> in 1245 he declared Emperor Frederick II deposed, and he sent envoys to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>. In the Baltic he authorised the coronation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindaugas">Mindaugas</a> as king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> in 1253 and reorganised the church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a>, uniting the diocese of Semigallia with Riga.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iv.jpg" alt="Pope Innocent IV" width="320" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Echando una mano · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:C_o_a_Fieschi_Popes.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sinibaldo Fieschi was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, the son of Beatrice Grillo and Ugo Fieschi, Count of Lavagna, of a noble merchant family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liguria">Liguria</a>. He studied at the universities of Parma and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> and was a canon of the Cathedral of Parma from 1216 to 1227. Regarded as one of the best canonists of his time, he wrote a commentary on papal decrees. Called to the Roman Curia by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-honorius-iii">Pope Honorius III</a>, he rose quickly, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-ix">Pope Gregory IX</a> made him Cardinal-Priest of San Lorenzo in Lucina on 18 September 1227. He was papal governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-of-ancona">March of Ancona</a> from 1235 to 1240.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After a year and a half of contentious debate, the conclave elected Fieschi on 25 June 1243. He accepted reluctantly, having been on friendly terms with Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a>. Negotiations failed over the return of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> to the Patrimony of St Peter. Finding himself unsafe in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, Innocent fled on 7 June 1244 by way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>, where he arrived on 29 November.</p><p>The First Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> met in three public sessions in June and July 1245, with about 150 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, mostly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. It ended on 17 July with the solemn deposition and excommunication of Frederick, and the release of his subjects from their allegiance. The conflict eased only with Frederick's death in December 1250. Innocent left Lyon in April 1251 and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in October 1253. On 15 May 1252 he issued the bull Ad extirpanda, which advised civil authorities to treat heretics as criminals and authorised torture to compel confessions.</p><p>He saw himself as Vicar of Christ, with power above earthly kings. In 1245 he sent Giovanni da Pian del Carpine to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>; the Khan Güyük replied in 1246 demanding the submission of the Pope and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s rulers. On 23 September 1243 the bull Qui iustis causis authorised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusades</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>. In 1247 he ordered that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Talmud</a> be censored rather than burned. He is often credited with developing the idea of legal personality, persona ficta.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In his last years Innocent worked to overthrow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manfred-king-of-sicily">Manfred of Sicily</a>, Frederick's natural son. On 14 May 1254 he invested Edmund Crouchback, the nine-year-old son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-of-england">Henry III of England</a>, with the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. He entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> on 27 October 1254, and learned there of Manfred's victory at Foggia on 2 December over papal forces. He died in Naples on 7 December 1254 and was succeeded by Alexander IV.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pope Innocent IV” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Seeking peace with the Livonian Order, he accepted baptism in 1251 and was crowned king in 1253 with the authority of <b>Pope Innocent IV</b>, the only crowned king Lithuania ever had.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Threatened by Emperor Frederick II, <b>Pope Innocent IV</b> arrived at Lyon.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-2">2 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1251 <b>Pope Innocent IV</b> united the diocese with Riga, which shortly afterwards became the Archbishopric of Riga.</blockquote><a href="/state/bishopric-of-semigallia">Bishopric of Semigallia</a></li><li><blockquote>To split his enemies he accepted baptism in 1251, and <b>Pope Innocent IV</b> authorised his coronation, which took place in July 1253, probably in Vilnius.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-lithuania">Kingdom of Lithuania</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Genoa, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.41,8.93&amp;year=1233" aria-label="Genoa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Pope Innocent IV"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5544 3760h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5544 3760h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.41,8.93&amp;year=1233">What was here in 1233? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
